    "summary": "Lost jobs rose 868 -\u003e 878: 10 accepted at a 10:11 full machine reboot, plus the unexplained 868 from 05:17; Pebble and RSS flat",
    "body": "Uptime and restarts: run started 17:00:29 UTC Oct 10, now about 43h20m. Kill -9s in /events: 23:18 Oct 10, then 02:24, 06:54, 10:45, 14:25, 18:31, 21:53 (Oct 11), 01:15, 05:20:46 (Oct 12). That is 9 kills. No kill since 05:20:46, so the gap is now about 7h, outside the 3h to 4.5h cadence. Nothing in /events explains it. A full machine reboot (\"reboot\" event at 10:11:48.198, not a kill): pid 805 became 654, uptime reset, target restarts counter went to 0 in the 10:00 hour. It is not clear whether this is the 36h restart (due about 05:00 from run start). Earlier unexplained pid replacements at 19:17 Oct 10 and 00:34 Oct 11 are still open.\n\nLEDGER since my 09:20 report: lost 868 -\u003e 878 (+10). This is NEW. The 10 jobs were accepted 10:11:48.411 to .414, about 200 ms after the 10:11:48.198 reboot. They are not in the kill flush window, and the lost_within_flush_window counter stayed at 46, so the classifier does not cover reboots. The 10:11 minute shows client_errors 89/s and the 10:12 minute 270/s, which fits a refused-request window. Earlier 868 (accepted 05:17:00 to 05:17:53, 20 listed in recent_lost) are unchanged. Their outage was the 05:16:58 webhook-down window (140 s) with upstream switching 429 -\u003e healthy at the same second, and the kill came 3.5 min later at 05:20:46. The 05:00 hour max_outstanding was 35,654 against a usual cap of about 10,000, the largest backlog in the run. Ledger reconciles to within the usual offset of about 3 against the 46 flush-window jobs. Other counters: duplicates 130 -\u003e 611 (the jump is in the 10:00 hour, after the reboot, so the reboot is the likely cause; within_10m_of_restart matches); finished_without_webhook 161,057 -\u003e 161,249 (+192); failed 698,996 -\u003e 701,808; stuck_over_24h 0; outstanding 5 at 12:19.\n\nTrends (43 hourly rows, contiguous):\n- Pebble: plateau since 22:00 Oct 11. Hour ends 819 to 942 MB in the last 14 h (884 MB at 11:00). The 1.6 to 1.9 GB peak of Oct 11 afternoon is not coming back. Not a leak.\n- RSS hour ends 219 to 237 MB, flat since 00:00 Oct 12. Heap in use about 125 to 190 MB.\n- Open fds at hour ends: 270 to 470 (Oct 12). Earlier 438 to 626. Peaks come from the 4,335-goroutine and 1,380-fd chaos windows and fall back. No climb.\n- Accept p99 healthy steady about 70 to 155 ms. E2E p99 is dominated by outage windows, so it is not usable as a trend.\n- Daily ramp: ceiling 750/s on Oct 11 and again on Oct 12 (04:00). 1000/s not sustained on either day. Not falling.\n\nHarness: 43 hourly rows contiguous. The last complete hour is 11:00. minutes_target_unreachable 0. No generator_saturated in the last 3h. One driver restart gap, 19:16:57 Oct 10 (seq 4,270,644 to 4,350,000). No new alerts other than the lost-jobs one. The status-page alert text is \"878 accepted jobs lost\".\n\nUnexplained: (1) why 868 jobs accepted at 05:17 were never terminal, and whether the 05:20 kill caused it or it was the webhook/upstream transition alone; (2) why the 10:11 reboot dropped 10 accepted jobs about 200 ms after the signal; (3) why no kill for 7h; (4) the 19:17 and 00:34 pid replacements and the kill-counter off-by-one. The /logs grep returns nothing for known lost job IDs, so the logs cannot be checked.\n\nNext: (1) get persistent logs or a job-level trace for 05:16 to 05:21 Oct 12 and 10:11:40 to 10:12:10 Oct 12; (2) add reboot to the flush-window classifier, or count reboot losses separately; (3) confirm whether 10:11 was the 36h restart and whether the kill schedule has stopped; (4) reproduce a webhook-down window that ends with an upstream recovery, followed by a kill, in a controlled run."
  },
